TITLE:: AuditorySceneAnalysis
summary:: High-level management of auditory objects and streaming.
categories:: Analysis
related:: Classes/ASAManager, Classes/MultiF0Tracker

DESCRIPTION::
AuditorySceneAnalysis (ASA) is the top-level controller for the tracking system. It implements the principles of Albert Bregman's "Auditory Scene Analysis," which describes how the human brain organizes sound into distinct "streams" or "objects."

This class coordinates the lower-level trackers (like MultiF0Tracker) and the historical database (MumpsPort) to maintain a coherent representation of the acoustic environment.

The system distinguishes between:
LIST::
## STRONG::Primitive Grouping::: Bottom-up grouping based on spectral commonalities (harmonics, onsets).
## STRONG::Schema-based Grouping::: Top-down grouping based on learned patterns (e.g., "that sounds like a human voice").
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Create a new AuditorySceneAnalysis instance.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

EXAMPLES::

code::
// Initializing the ASA system
a = ASAManager.new;
a.initSession(\demo);
a.recordAffordance(\demo, 0.5, 440, 0.95, \vocal);
::

THEORETICAL BACKGROUND::
Bregman's ASA theory posits that the auditory system uses "heuristics" to solve the source separation problem. For example, the strong::Common Fate:: principle suggests that frequency components that change in the same way (e.g., share the same vibrato) likely belong to the same source. This class implements these heuristics to group multiple voice tracks into a single "vocal ensemble" object.